  "articleBody": "Tile work in a bathroom looks straightforward until you consider what happens behind the surface. Installing ceramic or natural stone in wet areas requires precise waterproofing, structural stability, and substrate preparation to prevent costly leaks.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\n\n- Water in Showers and Wet Areas: Grout and tile are not waterproof barriers. Showers require an approved bonded waterproof membrane meeting ANSI A118.10 standards beneath the tile to prevent moisture from rotting framing.\n- Natural Stone Weight and Rigidity: Marble and slate demand a completely rigid, deflection-free subfloor and wall backing. An uneven substrate will quickly crack stone tiles or break bond lines.\n- Curbless and Custom Slope Drains: Shower pans must be pitched accurately toward the drain without flat spots that collect standing water.\n- Large-Format Tiles: Larger tiles require exceptionally flat walls and floors to avoid lippage, uneven grout joints, and hollow voids in the thin-set mortar.\n\nIf you notice spongy shower walls, cracked grout joints, or loose floor tiles, water may already be compromising the substrate. Fixing these issues correctly requires stripping down to the framing and building back up with proper backer boards, waterproofing membranes, and compatible setting materials.\n\nHH Remodeling LLC specializes in precision bathroom tile and marble installations throughout Aurora and Denver. Owner Abraham Becerra oversees every job on-site to ensure durable waterproofing and clean finishes. Call 303-505-0866 for an estimate.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}